{"t":1791317930,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"cb4bcdff-d861-4e6c-b45a-a0eceba82be8","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-06T18:47:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 061847\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n247 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNothing.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Quiet weather conditions are expected through the end of\n     the week.\n\n- 2) Increased rain potential late this weekend into early next\n     week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather conditions are expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure will shift offshore tonight allowing for a\nslightly warmer night. Winds turn southerly tomorrow allowing\ntemps to climb back into the 70s. Temperatures warm up to near\n80 Thursday before the passage of another dry cold front. Temps\ncool off slightly for the end of the week as weak high pressure\nof Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into early next \nweek. \n\nThe National Hurricane Center is currently monitoring a disturbance \nover the southwestern Gulf of America (AL92). It is anticipated that \nmoisture associated with what is now AL92 will move into our region \nlater this weekend or early next week. There are a lot of \npossibilities for where this system goes, and by extension, the\npotential impacts that could bring to our area. There will be a\nfew key features ahead of this system to watch, but the most \nimportant will be a cutoff low expected to develop over TX. \nCutoff lows are notoriously difficult to forecast, hence, the \nlevel of uncertainty with the forecast. At this juncture, \nseveral scenarios appear possible:\n\n(1) Quick-moving low pressure moves onshore of the Gulf Coast, and \nthreads the needle between high pressure over the Southwest Atlantic \nand the Upper Midwest, allowing it to move further north toward the \nOH Valley, central Appalachians and Mid-Atlantic, delivering notably \nhigher rain potential and amounts locally.\n\n(2) A very quick-moving low pressure system is forced to make an \neastward turn sooner across the Southeast prior to the western \nextent of SW Atlantic ridging exiting the coast. This would keep a \nmajority of moisture and rain chances to our south, though some \nmoisture could still be drawn northward along a passing front.\n\n(3) A slow-moving low pressure system is forced to make that \neastward turn sooner, blocked in this scenario by the second area of \nhigh pressure in the Upper Midwest building in before it can trek \ntoo far to the north. This would keep nearly all precipitation and \nmoisture associated with the low suppressed to the south.\n\n(4) A very slow-moving area of low pressure would not only get \ncaught beneath ridging building over the Upper Midwest, but could \nthen remain cutoff from the large scale steering pattern. This would \nleave low pressure meandering somewhere well to our south for a \ntime, before an uncertain exit beyond the current forecast period.\n\nAt the moment, deterministic and ensemble medium range guidance \nexhibits very large spread, falling into (but not necessarily \nfavoring) any one of the four scenarios listed above.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for details on any potential \nfurther development of AL92. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n\nVFR conditions are expected through the end of the week. Winds\nturn southerly Wednesday as high pressure shifts offshore. A dry\ncold front Thu night will cause another wind shift Thu night\ninto Fri. \n\nVFR conditions are expected on Friday and Saturday as high pressure \nremains dominant over the region. Winds will be out of the N or \nNW on Friday at around 5 to 10 knots, before switching out of \nthe S to SE on Saturday at the same speed. \n\nMore uncertainty going into Sunday, but sub-VFR conditions are \npossible if we do manage to get any rain associated with the area of \nlow pressure over the southwestern Gulf of America. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds are expected to strengthen Wednesday out of the south and\ncould flirt with marginal SCA conditions Wednesday evening. \n\nWinds are expected to remain below SCA criteria on Friday and \nSaturday as high pressure remains dominant over the region. However, \nby Saturday evening, we could see a brief burst of southerly \nchanneling winds that come close to criteria. Confidence is low at \nthis time. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/CJL\nAVIATION...LFR/CJL\nMARINE...LFR/CJL\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791317898,"days":{"2026-10-06":{"hi":66,"lo":47,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":71,"lo":55,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":79,"lo":56,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":73,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":72,"lo":57,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":69,"lo":62,"pop":64,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-12":{"hi":72,"lo":60,"pop":53,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791316800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[3,3,3,2,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,5,5,6,7,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,8,7,7,8,8,9,9,8,7,7,6,6,6,5,5,5,5,5,6,6,7,7],"gst":[4,4,4,3,3,3,4,4,4,4,4,4,5,5,5,6,6,7,8,9,10,10,10,10,1